Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||10-10-2000||10:23 PM||||Well people,<BR> The time has come to get the 3.2 into the fiero. And this being my first time puting a new engine into a car, I'd love any and all pointers you'd be willing to share! Thanks<P>-Rick||||reg||1
Z||000001||Uaana||10-10-2000||10:44 PM||||1. Double the estimated time<BR>2. Double the estimated cost <BR>3. Buy flowers for your significant other<BR>4. Ignore me (I'm just trying to get in more posts)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||10-11-2000||04:20 AM||||I'd have to agree with Uaana  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I have more posts than you!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-11-2000||07:30 AM||||I agree with Uaana..Plan on replacing more "other" parts.  What other? Thats what the extra cost will involve.  Just as well do the bushings, exhaust manifold probably cracked, alternator, water pump, new injectors, all new sensors, exhaust system.  As now the engine is apart, just as well do serious transmission work..new clutch for manual or overhaul the automatic.  Have fun!||||reg||3
Z||000004||mrfixit58||10-11-2000||07:44 AM||||While it's all apart, it's a good time to replace the axle seals, motor mounts and oil sending unit. Don't forget to grind out the restrictions in the exhaust manifolds.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||10-11-2000||08:39 AM||||I spent the majority of my time refurbishing and detailing the engine before I installed it.  I pressure washed and bead blasted and repainted everything.<P>I'd suggest taking the time to gasket match port the intake and exhaust manifolds.  It's not hard and you will like the results.  Also have the exhaust manifolds welded on the outside while they are off.<P>Run a tap in  all the holes in the block, etc. It is time consuming but worth the effort.<P>While the engine is out it is a good time to work on the A/C system.  If you need a new compressor replace all the o-rings in the system.  They aren't difficult to get to with the engine out.<P>You might as well replace the fuel filter now also.<P>I took a lot of time cleaning the gunk off the wiring harness.<P>Enjoy the project and take your time.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||10-11-2000||11:42 AM||||Unfortunatly time and money are somewhat limited. This car was my daily driver until a few months ago when the engine started knocking. And being an E-3 in the military isn't exactly living the rich life, so I've been scripming pretty hard just to get the engine out of the shop. I went threw and cleaned out the engine bay, but as for the 'replacement' stuff, I'm afraid injectors, water pump, welding work, etc are currently on the edge up the budget. I'd like to do the exhaust porting, but with the huge shop I have access to, there is no dremel.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I'm a little worried about the altinator, it squeaked a bit when I turned it by hand, hopefully is was just a little surface rust on the bearings. Water pump and engine mounts all looked good, they were all replaced about 20k miles ago.<P>I'm debating just removing the AC compressor, just because there's no freon in it, and with T-Tops, I'm not too worried about it (and it'll make the engine reinstalation that much easier!!)<P>Basically for new stuff, it's an indepth tune up, air and fuel filters, temp sending unit, spark plugs, wires, rotor, cap, etc...<P>Things I was wondering about were things like suggested break in. old engines I've always hear 2k RPMs for 20 minutes, the only problem is I've seen lots of cars have over heating problems in those 20 minutes, and with the complexity of the fiero coolant system, I was wonding if there were any tricks for making it go easier.<P>any other prestart/break in suggestions? pre oiling technecs? or other things to watch for?<P>As far as tapping all the holes, the block is coming back from a pro rebuild, it's been hot tanked and everything, would tapping really do anything?||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||10-11-2000||07:12 PM||||I am rebuilding and blueprinting a 3.4-liter Camaro engine for my Formula and I have to agree with Uaana about costs and time. You have to tap every stinking hole in your block so that you can get true torque readings when you torque everything down. The most IMPORTANT thing though is CLEANLYNESS. You MUST clean everything thoroughly until it is totally spotless. It should take you about 6 hours to properly clean a block. If you wipe a clean white rag through the cylinders and get any signs of grit than you’re not done. Use Soap and water to clean the block. I have rebuilt two engines in the past and have training in the automotive trade.<P><BR>You probably already know this but I wouldn't want to see someone go through all that expense to have there engine last 20,000 miles and then crap out. Not cleaning the engine enough is on of the biggest mistakes people make when rebuilding an engine. Hope this helps you.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||10-11-2000||11:12 PM||||okay, maybe I didn't phrase this correctly... the engine is assembled from oilpan to lower intake. hot tanked, cleaned and painted. all work done by one of the most reputable shops in the Northern VA area. heads are on, valve lash is adjusted. <P>Intake, distributer, water pump, and wireharness and it'll be in the car...||||reg||1
